WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.760
From small LAN tournaments and niche player bases,

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.900
to sold out stadiums and leagues that may one day rival those of traditional sports.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.200
Esports has come a long way.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.380
But what are some specific moments we can point to, that helped shape the esports landscape

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:21.480
as we know it today.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.800
To be frank, that's hard to trace. And even harder to rank.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.800
But we here at theScore esports, we're up for the challenge.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:36.260
So after weeks of heated debate here in the office, we give you the Top 10 Definitive Moments in Esports

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:36.960
History.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:45.400
Okay guys we know that you’ve probably seen this play once or twice, but that doesn’t

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.720
make it any less significant.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:52.940
So as you may have guessed, the world-famous Faker Ryu Zed outplay is kicking off our list

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:53.960
at number 10.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:01:03.360
(Casting)

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.540
By now, we shouldn’t have to explain why this moment is so important to esports, but

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:11.460
for those of you who don’t know, there was a point in time when the west wasn’t too

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:16.680
familiar with Korean League of Legends or a young prodigy named Faker.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:23.600
"All aboard the Korean Hype Train, SK Telecom T1."

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.860
In a single moment of mechanical brilliance and perfect understanding of the game, Faker

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.660
not only declared to the world that he is one of the best players to ever pick up the

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.800
game, but shattered the game’s mechanical ceiling.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.800
(Casting)

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:45.620
The play went viral in the West and was the moment when the world truly understood that

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.040
South Korea was playing the game on a different level.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.980
In its infancy, esports was primarily observed as a part of South Korean culture but in the

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:02.350
year 2000, the World Cyber Games was formed in an attempt to emulate a traditional

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.030
international sporting event.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:09.539
That’s why our number 9 spot celebrates the formation of WCG and the idea of esports

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:11.520
as a global competition.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:17.120
"The fastest mouse clickers in the country are in Sydney this weekend, hoping to qualify as Australia's

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:19.680
entrants in the World Cyber Games in October."

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.240
With the goal of bringing countries together to compete in multiple titles, the event had

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.980
been heralded as the Olympics of esports, and received sponsorship from the likes of

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.580
Samsung, Microsoft as well as branches of the South Korean government.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:39.640
In fact, in the early 2000s, WCG was considered the biggest event of the year for many western

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:40.620
pro players.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.160
"The WCG has grown to become the world's largest gaming festival.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.640
with more than one million participants in a year round tournament spanning 67 countries."

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.980
While it wasn’t the only esports event in the world to feature

00:02:52.989 --> 00:02:58.470
players from different countries competing, WCG played a huge role in turning esports

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:04.290
into a competition that transcends geographical boundaries and it paved the way for the global

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.900
competition we’ve come to expect today.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:14.920
Who would have thought that we'd ever be watching esports in major sports stadiums?

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:19.060
That’s why our number 8 moment takes us back to a time when the thought of selling

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:21.720
out a sports stadium felt like a dream.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.640
While tournaments had been held in famous exhibition spaces, the League of Legends Season 2

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.060
World Championship in 2012 marked the first time in which we saw an esports event take

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:37.000
place in a major North American sports venue, having sold out in just about a week.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:43.440
"10,000 adoring fans were in attendance. All to to see who will be crowned Season 2 World Champion."

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:45.980
And though the Galen Center may seem small in

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.560
hindsight, Season 2 Worlds set the bar for future esports venues.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:58.160
"Good evening and welcome to the 2016 League of Legends World Championship Semifinal

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.160
broadcasting live from New York's Madison Square Garden."

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.460
And since then, we’ve seen a number of historic venues open their doors for an esport event.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:15.960
With the growth of the League of Legends esports scene, Riot has begun to transform their competitive

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:21.980
leagues to operate under a franchise model, a huge turning point for modern esports that

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:23.920
takes our number 7 spot.

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:27.220
"And fundamentally we believe that all parties that contribute to an ecosystem,

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:28.520
should also benefit from it.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:32.840
So to do so, we'll be sharing League revenues with teams and pros beginning in 2018."

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:39.500
China’s LPL was the first major competitive
region to adopt a franchise approach in 2017,

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:44.780
while the NA LCS followed a year later, which meant security and stability for the players

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:47.140
as well as investors and team owners.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.140
But it wasn’t just Riot.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:54.580
Blizzard announced plans for a franchised Overwatch league shortly after the game’s initial release.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:59.140
"Together with our new team owners, we are working towards launching the Overwatch League,

00:04:59.140 --> 00:05:05.500
as the pinnacle of Overwatch esports. One that all aspiring players and audiences can celebrate

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:07.600
strive towards, and call their own."

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:13.100
Franchising in esports is still in its early stages and it is too soon to say what effects

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.900
it will have in the long run.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:19.280
But the money is there and the promise of stability has shown that big-name investors

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:24.180
are not scared to get into the space and elevate it to heights that were once considered a pipe

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:24.780
dream.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:32.780
2017 was a turning point for League of Legends’ competitive landscape.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:39.480
By now you’ve undoubtedly heard of SKT, often regarded as the greatest LoL team in the world.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:46.020
But Samsung Galaxy put an end to their dynasty, besting SKT in a Worlds grand final that denied

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.560
them a third consecutive Worlds title

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:59.680
"The SKT Dynasty is over, all hail the new kings, Samsung Galaxy! Your 2017 World Champions."

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.240
SKT swept Worlds 2013 finals in dominant fashion.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:09.420
After missing the World Championship in 2014, they would go on to reach back to back MSI

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:12.060
finals, winning in 2016.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:19.880
"And SK Telecom continue to rule the world, and they are your Mid-Season Invitational Champions."

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:26.060
They would also take home consecutive World Championships from 2015 to 2016, cementing

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:31.120
their status of being the undisputed greatest team in League of Legends.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:39.000
"SK Telecom have won every World Championship they have entered, they are our first

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.560
defending World Champions."

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:46.640
The entire Korean scene revolved around this organization who helped establish a period

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:52.680
of dominance unheard of in any team-based esport and players around the world strived

00:06:52.700 --> 00:06:54.780
to follow in their trail.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:07:00.760
SKT defined an era of excellence and although it may have come to an end, their legacy remains

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:05.740
the gold standard in esports for what it means to be the greatest and most dominant team

00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:07.100
on the planet.

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:15.760
CS:GO may be one of the world’s biggest esports, but it wasn’t always that way as

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:21.860
the competitive scene was split between CS:GO and CS 1.6 at the time of the game's release.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:27.340
That’s why the number 5 moment on our list brings us back to 2015, a year that cemented

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:30.760
CS:GO as the definitive first person shooter title

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.680
and an esports juggernaut.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:41.800
(Casting)

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:47.040
Valve had already shown that they were willing to get involved and help it grow with the sponsorship

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:48.940
of the Major Championships.

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:55.440
ESL One: Katowice was the first Major of 2015 and not only was the competition incredible,

00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.540
the event was record breaking.

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:04.820
Across Twitch, over 850,000 fans tuned in for the finals, a 150% viewership increase

00:08:04.820 --> 00:08:07.740
since ESL One: Cologne 2014.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:16.860
"This is what esports is about. It's unreal, we're grateful players. I never in my life been so happy."

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:17.900
"It's amazing."

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:22.940
While League of Legends was still at the top of the esports landscape, CS:GO had shot its

00:08:22.940 --> 00:08:27.560
way up and showed no signs of slowing down with more records being shattered at

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.040
ESL: One Cologne 2015.

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:40.240
(Casting)

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:45.400
But its growth was not exclusive to viewership--investors were flocking to the scene as well.

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:50.070
More teams were being signed to big professional organizations and leagues with huge sums of

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:52.000
prize money were announced.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:57.340
"Hi I'm Shaquille O'Neal, part owner of the NRG. When it comes to Counter-Strike and ELEAGUE?

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:00.020
I wanted to get deep. Deep into the game."

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:05.200
As CS:GO was propelled to the forefront, the first person shooter genre found its place

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:07.560
in the world of modern esports.

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:18.800
Taking the number 4 spot is one of the biggest esports rivalries of all time, Flash vs. Jaedong.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:23.200
The professional StarCraft scene in Korea had long been established since the earliest

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:29.360
days of televised esports, but by 2007, that pro scene was beginning to fade.

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:33.700
The next generation of stars had stepped up to make a name for themselves but none managed

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:38.360
to achieve the level of dominance that previous players like BoxeR had attained.

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:44.200
"Boxer vs freaking YellOw. The legends match to end all legends matches."

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:49.060
But with each recurring encounter between Flash and Jaedong, StarCraft was thrown into

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:54.460
a new age, better than any that had ever come before bringing some of the biggest crowds

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.400
esports had ever seen.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:08.960
(Casting)

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:13.380
Flash would achieve an incomprehensible level of dominance becoming the greatest player

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:17.720
to ever touch a game that was thought to have been completely solved at the time.

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:22.720
"And Flash has gotta be so stoked, there he goes out. Taking the golden mouse, grasping it

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:26.920
off of its pedestal. Thrusting it triumphantly into the air."

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:31.460
With multiple championships under his belt in just about every prestigious tournament

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:36.360
there was, his otherworldly level of play was only rivaled by Jaedong.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:40.640
A match between Flash and Jaedong was a Clash of Gods.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:45.040
The two always took the game to its absolute limits, to a point that has yet to be seen

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:50.800
in any other esport, and redefined the concept of greatness, reshaping the world’s understanding

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:54.660
of not only StarCraft but of competition itself.

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:02.720
When we think of Dota 2 esports, we think of The International.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:06.920
(Casting)

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:10.980
And when we think of The International, we can’t help but think of its multi-million

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:12.740
dollar prize pools.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:17.300
Which is why the compendium finds itself at the number three spot on our list.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:23.180
With a base prize pool of $1.6 million, Valve had introduced the Compendium for the International’s

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:24.420
third iteration.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:30.980
It cost $10 and was available to be purchased more than once to help unlock in-game rewards.

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:36.400
Most importantly, 25% of the compendium’s proceeds went directly into the prize purse,

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:42.180
and through a crowdfunded effort, TI3 smashed the record for the largest esports prize pool

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:46.800
at a single event, totaling $2.87 million.

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:52.680
"It all comes down to this, one more game, two million dollars on the table.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:12:00.020
Winner, first place at The International, the Aegis, and the prestige of being the best Dota 2 team in the world."

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:03.740
But then came The International 2014.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:09.680
Using the same Compendium system as the previous year but with added incentives, TI4’s crowdfunded

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:17.140
prize pool had more than tripled the previous TI’s, with first place taking home $5 million.

00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:20.480
And let’s not even get started on the most recent TI.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:26.620
"Over the years the prizepool's increased from 1.6 million to 3 million, to 10 million, 15 million, and the most recent

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:29.260
one having just under 25 million dollars."

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:33.940
Other esports titles and game developers would follow in Valve’s footsteps.

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:40.360
Riot for example crowdfunded their 2016 Worlds prize pool by selling in-game cosmetics, bringing

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:46.500
the prize up to a total of $5.07 million, which ended up more than doubling the base

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:49.060
amount of $2.13 million.

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:54.620
Blizzard’s introduction of the War Chest for StarCraft II in 2017 added an additional

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:58.940
$200,000 to the base prize pool of $500,000.

00:12:59.080 --> 00:13:03.120
Fans have shown that they are eager to help contribute to prize pools in exchange for

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:07.680
in-game rewards, proving the model to be a success for esports.

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:17.500
EVO Moment 37--a moment so incredible that it transcends the world of fighting games.

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:22.260
This timeless classic takes the runner up spot on our list and deservedly so.

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:27.340
In 2004 there was no Twitch, there were no million dollar prize pools, and for most people

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:30.380
competing in video games was merely a hobby.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:35.800
The hundreds of attendees were all there for the love of the games, and there is no moment

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:40.740
in esports that rivals the level of pure passion seen in Moment 37.

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:44.060
"You know everyone kept talking about how great he is and everything like that,

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:50.940
But the Daigo parry was that one moment where it happened. And it happened in front of a bunch of people

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:52.320
and it was ridiculous."

00:13:52.420 --> 00:13:54.400
Moment 37 had it all.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:59.320
Two players of different nationalities battling their hearts out for a chance at glory.

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:04.860
The home crowd favourite against a foreign legend, locked in an intense psychological war.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:10.620
And in the blink of an eye, a never before seen display of unquestionable mechanical

00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:15.880
prowess lead to one of the greatest comebacks ever witnessed, captured under the deafening

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:20.680
roar of a crowd who had just seen the impossible made into reality.

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:29.000
Even those who don’t understand Street Fighter can appreciate its brilliance.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:33.280
The clip had gone viral in a time when that was no easy feat and remains one of the

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:36.300
most watched esports moments of all time.

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:41.840
Moment 37 is everything that esports should be and to this day continues to embody the

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:43.720
spirit of competitive gaming.

00:14:46.820 --> 00:14:52.200
What started as a guy broadcasting his life in 2007, turned into the most revolutionary

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:58.140
esports moment of all time, which is why we’re crowning the birth of Twitch.tv as our number

00:14:58.160 --> 00:14:59.040
one moment.

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:03.460
"We spent about six months working on Justin.tv gaming, and getting quite good results

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:09.380
before realizing we needed a new brand. And we relaunched that brand as Twitch.

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:10.680
In 2011."

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:17.520
In June 2011, Justin.tv had launched their dedicated streaming platform for gamers--Twitch.tv.

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:22.100
While Justin.tv was not the first and only platform available for streaming video games,

00:15:22.100 --> 00:15:27.060
the decision to split off from the main site and rebrand coincided with the global boom

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:29.400
of esports that was happening at the time.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:32.980
"StarCraft II had just come out and that was pretty popular and people were uploading

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:36.660
videos to YouTube and it was like, we should try and get more of this content on the site."

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:41.700
And there is no doubt that Twitch helped esports grow even bigger with its accessibility.

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:45.980
While companies like MLG had been broadcasting tournaments on their own platform for years

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:50.880
before Twitch, the name had become so closely associated with esports that fans would not

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:52.600
settle for anything else.

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:57.580
If there was a tournament taking place, chances are you would watch it on Twitch and if there

00:15:57.580 --> 00:16:01.920
wasn’t, then you could be watching your favourite pros at home, or really just about

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:04.280
anyone playing anything you could think of.

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:12.600
(Stream)

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:17.829
Twitch had become an outlet not just for esports but for pros and entertainers alike to share

00:16:17.829 --> 00:16:21.620
their gameplay, grow their fan bases and grow the industry.

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:27.340
Esports was being broadcasted around the world, 24/7 for free.

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:32.920
In 2014, Amazon announced that it had acquired Twitch.tv for nearly a billion dollars and

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:36.600
since then, live streaming and esports have only gotten bigger.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:39.520
"Hang on, there's nothing hotter in streaming video."

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:42.540
"Yeah I think that's the case, and I really think that the-"

00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:43.560
"Including everything?"

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:45.920
"Including everything."

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:51.760
Simply put Twitch has cemented itself as not only a titan of live streaming but exemplifies

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:57.760
the ideal esports viewer experience and who knows where the industry would be without it.

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:03.760
Well guys that's our list, and let me tell you this was one of the hardest we've put together.

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:09.320
If we missed your personal favourite moment in esports history, let us know in the comments and make

00:17:09.320 --> 00:17:11.440
your argument and I'll see you next week.

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:16.120
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

